`research/target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_eight_noroot_k6_reduction_2026-09-22.md`.

## 1. Input from the current `k=6` reduction

The coefficient-constraint space in the eight-no-root `k=6` layer has rank two.

The preceding reduction leaves only

```text
A: D=-2, d_root=0, p=8,
B: D=-1, d_root=0, p=9.
```

Branch A is already forced to

```text
d_v=2,
q=8,
eight singleton changed blocks.
```

Moreover, every three-clean deletion `i` produces a seven-row rank-two state with

```text
D_i=0,
p_i=8,
d_i=2,
q_i>=7,
singleton capacity >=7,
```

and every one- and two-coordinate puncture of its coefficient space still has rank two.

Branch B has exactly the remaining global profiles

```text
(d_v,q,singleton_min) =
(1,7,5), (1,8,7),
(2,8,7), (2,9,9), (3,9,9).
```

The goal here is to strengthen all of these profiles using replacement functionals rather than only counting compatible incomplete blocks.

## 2. Rank-uniform replacement-functional accounting

Fix a virtual-canonical state with

- `t` no-root rows;
- coefficient-constraint rank `r`;
- `c` virtual-complete root blocks;
- virtual-complete deficiency `d`;
- `p` selected root rows in incomplete blocks.

Write

```text
n = t-r.
```

The canonical relation matrix has `c+n` rows: one complete-block relation row for each virtual-complete block and `n` canonical external-lift rows from a basis of the coefficient nullspace. External containment puts every canonical external relation in the complete-block relation span, so this matrix has rank `c-d`.

Its row-index functional quotient therefore has dimension

```text
(c+n) - (c-d) = n+d = t-r+d.          (2.1)
```

Now replace `q` virtual-complete blocks by compatible incomplete states. For each such block, compare its local coefficients with the canonical complete coefficients on every nullspace basis vector. The difference is the usual row-index replacement functional. This is the same construction used by the committed replacement-functional audits; the linear algebra does not depend on coefficient rank one.

If the chosen replacement functionals gain extra rank `s` modulo the column space of the canonical relation matrix, then the relation rank after the replacements is

```text
(c-d) - q + s.
```

The root-row count contributed by unchanged virtual-complete blocks and selected incomplete rows is `3(c-q)+p`. Hence, before harmless completions of virtual-empty blocks,

```text
D
 = t + p + 3(c-q) - 3[(c-d)-q+s]
 = t + p + 3d - 3s.                  (2.2)
```

Incomplete states on virtual-empty blocks have zero quotient demand on the coefficient nullspace, and completing additional virtual-empty blocks cannot lower defect. Thus the general necessary bound is

```text
D >= t + p + 3d - 3s.                (2.3)
```

Combining (2.1) and (2.3), `s<=t-r+d`; whenever the defect forces the opposite inequality, the replacement functionals must fill the entire quotient.

## 3. Branch A globally requires eight independent replacement functionals

For branch A,

```text
t=8, r=2, D=-2, p=8, d=2.
```

Equation (2.1) gives quotient dimension `8-2+2=8`. Equation (2.3) gives

```text
-2 >= 8 + 8 + 3*2 - 3s = 22 - 3s,
```

so `s>=8`. Therefore

```text
s=8.                                  (3.1)
```

The preceding reduction already forces `q=8` and all eight changed virtual-complete blocks to be singletons. An actual state supplies exactly eight replacement functionals, so (3.1) says those eight functionals must be linearly independent modulo the canonical relation column space.

This is strictly stronger than singleton capacity eight. If `s<=7`, (2.3) gives `D>=1`, contradicting `D=-2`.

## 4. Every three-clean seven-row shadow must fill rank seven

Take any three-clean coordinate from branch A. The preceding reduction proves

```text
t=7, r=2, D=0, p=8, d=2.
```

The functional quotient has dimension `7-2+2=7`. The defect bound gives

```text
0 >= 7 + 8 + 3*2 - 3s_7 = 21 - 3s_7,
```

so

```text
s_7=7.                                (4.1)
```

If `q_7=7`, the seven replacement functionals from the virtual-complete incomplete blocks must all be independent modulo the seven-row canonical relation column space. If `q_7=8`, the eight chosen replacement functionals must span the full seven-dimensional quotient.

Thus capacity at least seven is only a combinatorial prefilter. The actual finite target is full extra rank seven.

The first exact computation should enumerate the depth-two-synchronized seven-row rank-two `d=2` states, construct every compatible singleton replacement functional, and discard any state whose over-approximating singleton-functional span has extra rank at most six. Inserting every compatible singleton-state functional simultaneously is safe: an actual root state chooses at most one state per block, so the over-approximation can only increase span.

A full-cover result

```text
max_seven_singleton_extra_rank <= 6
```

would close branch A. If rank-seven seven-row states exist, only those survivors need to be extended to eight rows, where the eight actual singleton replacement functionals must have extra rank eight.

## 5. Every surviving branch-B global profile is also full-quotient

For branch B,

```text
t=8, r=2, D=-1, p=9.
```

For any surviving deficiency `d`, equation (2.3) gives

```text
-1 >= 8 + 9 + 3d - 3s,
```

hence `s>=6+d`. Equation (2.1) gives quotient dimension `8-2+d=6+d`. Therefore every surviving branch-B profile satisfies

```text
s=6+d.                                (5.1)
```

For the five profiles:

```text
(d,q)=(1,7): seven replacement functionals must be independent;
(d,q)=(1,8): eight replacement functionals must span rank seven;
(d,q)=(2,8): eight replacement functionals must be independent;
(d,q)=(2,9): nine replacement functionals must span rank eight;
(d,q)=(3,9): nine replacement functionals must be independent.
```

So the entire remaining `D=-1,p=9` side is also a maximal-functional-rank problem. Capacity and root-cost filters remain useful prefilters, but no surviving profile can tolerate one rank of slack below its quotient dimension.

## 6. Exact next target

For branch A, the sharpened seven-row target is

```text
rank(P_7)=2,
all one- and two-coordinate punctures rank two,
d_7=2,
p_7=8,
q_7>=7,
compatible singleton capacity >=7,
extra singleton-functional rank = 7.
```

The existing synchronized rank-two machinery already computes virtual-canonical complete sets, compatible singleton states, exact coefficient projections, and complete-block relation ranks. The new endpoint is the row-index functional span. The implementation can reuse the replacement-functional construction from the committed five-row functional audits, with the five-dimensional nullspace of a seven-row rank-two coefficient space.

If that seven-row class is empty, branch A closes. If it survives, extend only the full-rank survivors and require global extra rank eight. The same functional code then attacks the five branch-B profiles at exact target ranks `7,7,8,8,9`.

No finite census is claimed here. The new contribution is the exact maximal-rank condition that every remaining `k=6` counterexample must satisfy.
